//
// AN UNTYPED LAMBDA TO A FREE SET IS REFUSED, NAMING THE MEMBERS AND THE CURE
// (`@FR-Disp-Hint`): a name with several definitions offers no parse hint, so `|x| { x }` has
// nothing to type `x` from; the call is refused at the lambda, naming the definitions and the
// typed spelling `fn(x: <type>) -> <ret> { … }` that cures it — never typed from the first
// member.  The attic holds the `self`-set shape (`1926`); this is the FREE set.  One refusal per
// file.  The body does not read `x`: a body that does draws a second, cascading error — the
// sibling `a-refused-lambda-that-reads-its-parameter-says-only-the-refusal.loft`.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; the refusal fired as expected on 18594597b,
//   interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names both members and the typed spelling.
// HOLDS: a typed lambda picks its member (`1926b` in the attic).
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot infer the type of lambda parameter 'x': `hl` is hl(fn(integer) -> integer) and hl(fn(text) -> text), so no single definition types the lambda — write its types, `fn(x: <type>) -> <ret> { … }`

fn hl(f: fn(integer) -> integer) -> text { "int {f(2)}" }
fn hl(f: fn(text) -> text) -> text { "text {f("a")}" }

fn main() {
  println(hl(|x| { 7 }));
}
